me{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ opposite the given angle $∠C.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{15} \\\\ AB & =15\\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =15\\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}) \\\\  & =15(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =11.820\\,16… \\\\  & ≈11.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079491 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079491 #edb/ref 1061 456177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079491 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9cbed7d-509a-59f0-97ea-b1d35e68ec34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079491 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079492 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079492 #edb/ref 1061 456178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079492 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98edd011-945b-5796-8b62-c13b85ef2542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079492 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079493 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079493 #edb/ref 1061 456182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079493 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "58045faf-9a82-5f92-abc0-84d7e97bab68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079493 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079494 #edb/ref 1061 456186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079494 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "537be7b4-9e58-5792-804e-96fda2b10d68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079494 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079639 #edb/ref 1064 "Trigonometric ratios can be used to solve for a missing side of a right triangle if one side is known along with the measure of an acute angle. However, the question of which ratio to use depends on what side is given and what side is unknown. \n\n![](images/8b/8b123fed4a9a42ceb69f3a6ff68ed89f27ae42294137f0cd12a9232d329d77b0.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, we are given an angle $\\theta = 42^\\circ,$ and the length of the hypotenuse is given but the length of the side opposite is unknown.\n\nSo, we need to choose the trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite side to the hypotenuse. This is sine, and now we can just set up the ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{42}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we simply solve for the unknown value $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{42}^{∘} & =\\frac{x}{8} \\\\ x & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡{42}^{∘} \\\\  & =8(0.669\\,13...) \\\\  & =5.353\\,04... \\\\  & ≈5.4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nNote that we used a calculator to evaluate $\\sin(42^\\circ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079639 #edb/ref 1065 2449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079639 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e320c0f6-5f85-56c6-a082-c880c7e97433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079639 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519001 #edb/ref 1080 "11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519001 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519001 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "072ae5a3-56fd-5113-989d-660f16471484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519047 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519047 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175