92186519506 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e95f0005-9800-5362-8d1e-5d401889c55d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519519 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519519 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519519 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519519 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/42/42d1bf23b4ce679748fb1c1765d568b0b32128155f3d6e7770a1b894b0888ded.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{LM}$ in terms of $\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09ccf4cc-8d66-55a0-964d-b04cc4bd149d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519519 #edb/ref 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{MN}$ opposite the given angle $m∠L$  and wish to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{LM}.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(m∠L) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{6}{LM} \\\\ LM\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =6 \\\\ LM & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519533 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519533 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519533 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/80/804c19a20b6f47707a07c7bdcee3cbd727eec2c270109e30eb55d949ace8edf0.png)\n\nCalculate the value of $BC$ to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ca7cacc-1a5f-515a-8db1-0999afb66a6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519533 #edb/ref 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AC}$ opposite to the given angle $∠B$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent $∠B.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘}) & =\\frac{2.91}{BC} \\\\ BC\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘}) & =2.91 \\\\ BC & =\\frac{2.91}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\frac{2.91}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.91}{(0.363\\,97…)} \\\\  & =7.995\\,15… \\\\  & ≈8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519547 #edb/ref 1071 "A triangle $△ABC$ has $m∠A={38}^{∘},$ $m∠B={90}^{∘},$ and $AC=18.$ Find $AB$ rounded to the nearest integer.\n\n$AB≈${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65457b1f-86ca-5676-81db-c0f19c1bbed5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519547 #edb/ref 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/d0/d097dca887930886a231b4b3a71e3236edcc25ec50a886f197a8c3111ff064fb.png)\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{18} \\\\ AB & =18\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =18\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) \\\\  & =18(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =14.184\\,19… \\\\  & ≈14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-54245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519553 #edb/ref 1071 "A triangle $△ABC$ has $m∠A={37}^{∘},$ $m∠B={90}^{∘},$ and $AC=25.$ Find $AB$ rounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75f7a455-462c-51e4-9b1a-b0d79be3c2a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519553 #edb/ref 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/70/70ac3cbbc7209f71bd8095bb725d1dbb847c827bdfdc8220c25115fb120532b4.png)\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{25} \\\\ AB & =25\\operatorname{cos}⁡({37}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =25\\operatorname{cos}⁡({37}^{∘}) \\\\  & =25(0.798\\,63…) \\\\  & =19.965\\,88… \\\\  & ≈20,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519601 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519601 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519601 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519601 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a9/a9c863e0cdb9788e06c527e17207e87ba2281ab21c9bd0836009ca518a15631a.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ rounded to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519601 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f70a7592-ca8c-59ec-84a8-2fae0d3e16cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519601 #edb/ref 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠C$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ opposite $∠C.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({56}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{9} \\\\ AB & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡({56}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡({56}^{∘}) \\\\  & =9(1.482\\,56…) \\\\  & =13.343\\,04… \\\\  & ≈13.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
